<comment datatype='string'>&lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=PHENYLACETATE&quot;&gt;Phenylacetate&lt;/a&gt; is a major intermediate in bacterial degradation of many aromatic compounds [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/11260461&quot;&gt;Luengo01&lt;/a&gt;]. Microbes can oxidize phenylacetate under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions (see also &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-1341&quot;&gt;phenylacetate degradation II (anaerobic)&lt;/a&gt;), and the first step in both pathways is the same - the activition of phenylacetate to &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-207&quot;&gt;phenylacetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; by a &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=MONOMER-12132&quot;&gt;phenylacetate-CoA ligase&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;p&gt;
Despite the fact that the existence of an aerobic pathway was reported back in 1955 [&lt;a href=&quot;/META/reference.html?type=CITATION-REFERENCE&amp;object=%5bKunita55%5d&quot;&gt;Kunita55&lt;/a&gt;], the exact chemistry, which is unusual for an aerobic pathway, was solved only in 2010 [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20660314&quot;&gt;Teufel10&lt;/a&gt;]. All of the pathway's intermediates remain in the form of CoA-thioesters, a characteristic typical of anaerobic degradation pathways. 
&lt;p&gt;
De-aromatization of the ring is achieved by activation of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-207&quot;&gt;phenylacetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; to a &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD0-2362&quot;&gt;2-(1,2-epoxy-1,2-dihydrophenyl)acetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, catalyzed by &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=ENZYME&amp;object=CPLX-8168&quot;&gt;ring 1,2-phenylacetyl-CoA epoxidase&lt;/a&gt;, a multicomponent oxygenase. The reactive non-aromatic epoxide is then isomerized to a seven-member &lt;i&gt;o&lt;/i&gt;-heterocyclic enol ether, &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD0-2363&quot;&gt;2-oxepin-2(3H)-ylideneacetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;, and the ring is cleaved. The rest of the pathway consists of &amp;beta;-oxidation steps leading to formation of the TCA cycle intermediates &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=ACETYL-COA&quot;&gt;acetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=SUC-COA&quot;&gt;succinyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/17259607&quot;&gt;Nogales07&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20660314&quot;&gt;Teufel10&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
